<Include xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Component GUIDs that are named twice: once by the component that installs the resource
       (Service.wxs) and once by the ComponentSearch that looks it up (InstallLocation.wxs).

       The pairing has to be structural rather than a comment asking the next editor not to change one side.
       Windows Installer resolves a previous version's install folder by these exact values, and it is the
       only fallback for installs made by a version predating the InstallPath registry key - so an edit on
       one side alone does not fail the build or any test. It makes the search return nothing, the recovery
       quietly stops working, and the next upgrade relocates the install and strands appsettings.json (with
       the only copy of the secret) at the old path. CI never installs a pre-key version, so nothing catches
       it.

       Both are deliberately the SAME GUID in the x64, x86 and arm64 packages - all three share an
       UpgradeCode and cannot coexist, and one GUID per resource is what lets the fallback find another
       architecture's install. (Contrast InstallLocationRegistry, which needs a GUID per architecture, since
       each of those writes a different key; see the note there.)
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <?define JasServiceComponentGuid = "A3AFD0C7-0249-4230-8F74-1A229AF71C2C" ?>
  <?define JasDataFolderComponentGuid = "9D1F73AD-383E-436A-8F1A-7C83CFC0A5A0" ?>

</Include>
